  "agent": "Q4DecodeSplitK",
  "question": "Does splitting the k range of each Q4 decode GEMV output across multiple workgroups (the GEMV analogue of split-K) with a separate reduction pass raise achieved bandwidth toward the 60 GB/s layout roof, and is the model-level gain worth the accumulation-order change?",
  "decision": "RECEIPT-ONLY NEGATIVE: land no source change. Split-K lowers the in-model decode chain from 40.9 GB/s to 27.4 / 24.2 / 15.5 GB/s at S=2/4/8 (+52.9% / +77.4% / +190% layer time), and every split count loses on every shape in isolation. The reduction pass alone costs 33-65 us wall per dispatch. Model-level legs were NOT run: the isolated result fails the bar by a wide margin (a >50% per-layer regression cannot become a >=3% model-level gain), so a wheel build and legs matrix would measure nothing not already decided, and the split deliberately changes the accumulation order, which would move the pinned canonical digests with zero performance upside. The env-gated variant stays on this branch as instrumentation only. Never merged.",
  "host": "jwm1-linux (<m1-host>), Apple M1 (G13G B1), linux-asahi 7.1.6.asahi1-1, installed fork driver mesa-honeykrisp-omarchy 26.3.0.devel.hk6f6afc8-1 (verified in-window by pacman -Q per window)",
  "timing_instrument": "host CLOCK_MONOTONIC around whole submits only (window scripts and bench both wall-anchored); no device-timestamp number is used anywhere (known ~2.07x undercount on this driver)",
  "work_branch": "wave/Q4DecodeSplitK @ 73bb1e4 (implementation; receipt lands in the next commit on the same branch)",
